<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salamiyah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Salamiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salamiyah is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> on the edge of the steppe. In the 9th century it was the secret headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> leadership, from where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a> left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> around 902–903 to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>. Resettled in 1849 by Ismailis with Ottoman permission, it is now the largest centre of Syria's Ismaili community.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salamiyah.jpg" alt="Salamiyah" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salamiyya,view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Salamiyah lies in a fertile plain on the edge of the Syrian steppe, 33 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and 45 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, at an average elevation of about 460 metres. It is administratively part of the Hama Governorate. In the Byzantine period it was a flourishing town called Salaminias or Salamias, linked by roads to Emesa (Homs), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusafa">Resafa</a>. Its bishop Julian attended the consecration of Patriarch Severus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in 512, and one dated inscription records the building of a church of the Theotokos in 604.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered Salamiyah in 636. Not long after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> came to power in 750, the dynast Salih ibn Ali and his descendants settled it, and his son Abdallah built its irrigation networks. In the early 9th century the great-grandson of Ja'far al-Sadiq, also named Abdallah, settled there concealing his identity as a merchant, and his palace became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> da'wa.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</a> was born in Salamiyah in 873 or 874, and his reforms led to a break with Hamdan Qarmat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> razed the town in 903 and massacred its inhabitants; al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> had left the year before and went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>.</p><p>Salamiyah then declined. It was captured by a Fatimid general in 1009, passed in 1092 to Tutush, brother of the Seljuk sultan Malikshah, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1174–1175, and was incorporated into the Mamluk empire after 1263. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> army was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Ghazan there in 1299.</p><p>Under early Ottoman rule, from 1517, it was a large ruin held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> emirs, and in the 1830s it was still uninhabited. In July 1849 Isma'il ibn Muhammad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadmus">Qadmus</a>, obtained a firman from Sultan Abdülmecid I to settle the site with his followers, who called it Mecidabad. Many Ismailis followed from the Jabal Ansariya, and by the end of the 19th century about 6,000 people lived there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Salamiyah had 66,724 inhabitants at the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and a little more than half of its residents are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a>. It is the largest population centre of Ismailis in the Arab world and holds the headquarters of the Ismaili Higher Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The mausoleum of the Ismaili imam Abdallah, rebuilt in 1088 according to an inscription, still stands, and the poet Muhammad al-Maghut was born there in 1934. The city is an agricultural centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salamiyah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nizari community remained in the region, notably around <b>Salamiyah</b> and Masyaf.</blockquote><a href="/state/nizari-ismailis-of-syria">Nizari Ismailis of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
